---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Comments from '../../components/Comments.astro';
import WangcaiSeries from '../../components/WangcaiSeries.astro';
const P = '/misc/oregon-2025/';
---
<BaseLayout title="Thanksgiving, the Long Way Round — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Thanksgiving 2025: five days by car from Southern California to Portland and back down to Napa, with Crater Lake in fresh snow, the Blue Pool on the McKenzie River, Mt. Hood over Trillium Lake, Portland at dusk and the sea stacks of the southern Oregon coast, with our dog Wangcai.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · November–December 2025</p>
  <h1>Thanksgiving, the Long Way Round</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Five days from Southern California up to Portland and back down to Napa, with Wangcai in the back seat.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>5 days · November 27 – December 1, 2025</strong><br />
    Southern California → the San Francisco Peninsula → I-5 overnight → Klamath Falls → Crater Lake → Bend →
    Tamolitch Falls → Mt. Hood → Portland → North Bend → Bandon → the Curry County coast → Napa<br />
    Two states · one caldera lake under fresh snow · one hot pot dinner in Bend · Mt. Hood twice in one day ·
    a coastline of sea stacks · one rainbow on the dashboard · one cream-colored dog
  </div>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-overview.jpg'}><img src={P + 'map-overview.jpg'} alt="Map of the whole trip: north from Southern California through the Bay Area and up I-5 to Klamath Falls and Crater Lake, on to Bend and Tamolitch Falls, Mt. Hood and Portland, then south down the Oregon coast past Bandon and back to Napa, ending at Oakland airport." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>The whole trip, in the order the photos were taken.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Many of our long weekends end up in the car, and this one was a particularly long one. Thanksgiving gave us four
    days and a Monday, and we spent them driving from Southern California all the way up to Portland, Oregon, and
    then most of the way back, as far as Napa. That is the length of California and the length of Oregon, up the
    inland side and back down the coast. In between came a volcano's crater full of the bluest water in the country,
    a mountain lake dusted with the season's snow, a city at dusk with a volcano on its horizon, and a morning of
    sea stacks on the southern Oregon coast.
  </p>
  <p>
    As with the other trip posts here, everything below is rebuilt from the camera roll, where each photo carries a
    time and a place. This album is a small one, under sixty pictures for five days, and it is lopsided. Some hours
    got a dozen photos, and one whole day got none, so that day is told from memory instead. Where a place has a story
    worth knowing, I've added a little of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The trip also had a passenger in the back seat: <strong>Wangcai</strong>, our cream-colored dog, who
    <a href="/misc/wangcai-2025">came home with us</a> in January 2025 and has since become a regular on these
    drives. She rode the whole loop, there and back. She appears below at an overlook in Bandon, and
    again at the very end, asleep, which is the most accurate review of the trip anyone could write.
  </p>

  <h2>Day 1: Thanksgiving on I-5</h2>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-1127.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-1127.jpg'} alt="Map of the route: Days 1 and 2, from the San Francisco Peninsula north on I-5 through Shasta County to Klamath Falls, then up to Crater Lake." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Days 1–2: up I-5 into the night, then Crater Lake.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    We set out on Thanksgiving Day itself, November 27, and the first leg was the long, flat one. Thanksgiving dinner
    was in the late afternoon at Hubei Restaurant in Millbrae, on the San Francisco Peninsula, which cooks the food of
    my hometown, Wuhan, the real thing rather than a version of it. A plate of home on the way out of town is about as
    good a Thanksgiving as I can think of. Then we turned north. From the Bay Area, I-5 runs for hours up the Sacramento Valley, past orchards and
    rice fields that are invisible after dark, until the valley narrows at Redding and the road starts to climb toward
    Mount Shasta and the Oregon line.
  </p>

  <h3>A rainbow on the dashboard</h3>
  <p>
    The only photos from that stretch were taken just south of Redding, near Cottonwood, at 9:22 at night. They are
    not of the road but of the car's screen. Tesla hides a few jokes in its software, and one of them is the
    "Rainbow Road", a nod to the famous final track in Mario Kart: the path the car plans for itself down the lane is
    drawn not in the usual blue but as a glowing rainbow stripe. On a dark, nearly empty interstate on Thanksgiving
    night, with nothing outside the windows but the occasional pair of taillights, it was the most colorful thing for
    a hundred miles.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'rainbow-road.jpg'} alt="A car's dashboard screen showing the lane ahead drawn as a glowing rainbow stripe on a dark highway." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Rainbow Road, I-5 in Shasta County, 9:22 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Stars over the Klamath Basin</h3>
  <p>
    A little after midnight we were across the state line and just outside <strong>Klamath Falls</strong>, Oregon.
    The Klamath Basin sits at over four thousand feet on the dry, eastern side of the Cascades, a wide country of
    marshes, lakes and ranchland with very few lights in it, and on a clear late-November night the sky there is full.
    Between midnight and one in the morning, the camera roll holds a run of ten photos pointed straight up: black pines
    at the edge of the frame and stars scattered across the rest. After a long night of interstate, it was a good way
    to end the day.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'klamath-stars.jpg'} alt="A dark night sky scattered with stars above the silhouette of a pine tree." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Just past midnight near Klamath Falls.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Day 2: Crater Lake in the snow</h2>
  <p>
    From Klamath Falls it is about an hour north to <strong>Crater Lake National Park</strong>, and by 1:40 in the
    afternoon we were standing at the rim. In winter most of the park closes. The road that circles the rim is left to
    the snow, and the only way in is the south entrance, which is plowed up to <strong>Rim Village</strong>, at about
    7,100 feet. That turns out to be enough. From the promenade at Rim Village, the whole lake opens up below you at
    once, and there is no warming up to it: one step you are in the trees, and the next you are looking straight down
    into the crater.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'crater-lake-wizard.jpg'} alt="Crater Lake from the rim: deep blue water ringed by gray crater walls, with the forested cone of Wizard Island, snow and evergreens in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Crater Lake and Wizard Island from Rim Village.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'crater-lake-rim.jpg'} alt="A snowy slope with tall mountain hemlocks and a rock wall, looking down to Crater Lake and Wizard Island." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hemlocks and fresh snow along the rim.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The lake is the remains of a mountain. About 7,700 years ago, a volcano called Mount Mazama erupted so violently
    that it emptied the chamber beneath it, and the summit collapsed into the hole. Over the centuries that followed,
    rain and snow filled the bowl. No river flows in and none flows out, which is why the water is so clear and why
    its blue is unlike any other lake's. It is also the deepest lake in the United States, at 1,943 feet. Out in the
    water sits <strong>Wizard Island</strong>, a younger cinder cone that grew up inside the crater after the
    collapse, its top still dented with a small crater of its own.
  </p>
  <p>
    The park is one of the snowiest inhabited places in the country, averaging over forty feet a year, and the season
    was just getting started. A thin, fresh layer covered the slopes below the rim and the rocks along the wall, with
    dry grass still poking through. The sky was a clean, pale blue, and the far walls of the
    crater stood out sharp and gray, streaked with the first white of winter. The mountain hemlocks at the edge lean
    out over the drop as if they were looking too.
  </p>

  <h2>Day 3: Bend, and the Blue Pool</h2>
  <p>
    Saturday, the 29th, left no photos at all, but it was not an empty day. We spent it in <strong>Bend</strong>,
    the high-desert town on the dry side of the Cascades, where the Deschutes River runs through the middle of town
    and the ponderosa pines give way to sagebrush a few miles east. Around noon we wandered a shopping village on the
    edge of town, with an REI, a Lululemon and a bakery, the kind of slow, aimless browsing that a long drive makes
    you crave.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then I got a migraine, and the afternoon went on without me. The others drove west into the mountains to
    <strong>Tamolitch Falls</strong>, on the McKenzie River, for a hike, while I lay back in the car and waited for it
    to pass. The name is a little misleading: most of the year there is no waterfall there at all. The McKenzie
    disappears underground a few miles upstream, into the porous rock of an old lava flow, and comes back up through
    the bottom of a rock basin, so cold and clear that the pool it fills is an almost unreal turquoise. Everyone calls
    it the <strong>Blue Pool</strong>. I have only their word for the color.
  </p>
  <p>
    By the time they came back down the trail, my head had cleared. We drove back to Bend for dinner at Sen Hot Pot,
    which on a cold late-November night in the mountains was exactly right, and then back to the hotel.
  </p>

  <h2>Day 4: Mt. Hood and Portland</h2>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-1130.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-1130.jpg'} alt="Map of the route: Day 4, from Trillium Lake below Mt. Hood west to the Portland West Hills, then south to North Bend on the coast." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 4: Mt. Hood in the morning, Portland at dusk, then south to the coast.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Trillium Lake</h3>
  <p>
    Sunday morning found us at <strong>Trillium Lake</strong>, on the south side of Mt. Hood. It is a small lake,
    made in 1960 by damming a creek in a high meadow, and it is famous for one view: the mountain rising straight up
    over the far shore and reflected, on still days, in the water. It was snowing when we got there, which at this
    latitude at the end of November is no surprise. Portland, an hour and a half away, sits at about 45.5° north,
    farther north than Portland, Maine, at about 43.7°; the West Coast's mild weather makes it easy to forget how far
    up the map you are. By eleven every rock on the shore wore a clean white cap, every fir along the far bank was
    frosted, and a band of cloud lay across the mountain's flanks so that only the summit showed above it.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'trillium-lake.jpg'} alt="Trillium Lake with snow-capped rocks on the shore, snowy firs along the far bank, and the white summit of Mt. Hood rising above a band of cloud." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Trillium Lake, with Mt. Hood above the clouds.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'trillium-fir.jpg'} alt="A small snow-laden conifer on the shore of a dark blue lake, with snowy rocks and paw prints in the snow." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The shore in fresh snow.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The water was not quite still enough for the famous reflection, a light breeze ruffling it into dark blue ripples,
    but the morning made up for it in other ways. The light was low and bright, the sky a deep cold blue, and the snow
    on the shore so new that it still showed every track across it, paw prints included. A young cedar right at the
    water's edge had caught more snow than it could hold and bent under it like a Christmas card. Mt. Hood itself, at
    11,249 feet the tallest mountain in Oregon, stayed half hidden all morning, which is what it usually does.
  </p>

  <h3>Portland from the West Hills</h3>
  <p>
    By late afternoon we were on the other side of Portland, high in the <strong>West Hills</strong>, on a road just
    below Pittock Mansion. The mansion was built in 1914 for Henry Pittock, the publisher of <em>The Oregonian</em>,
    and he picked the site for this view: about a thousand feet above the city, looking east over downtown, the
    river and the suburbs to the Cascades beyond. It was 4:51, twenty minutes after sunset, and the light was almost
    gone from the city.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'portland-mt-hood.jpg'} alt="Downtown Portland at dusk seen from the forested West Hills, with the snow-covered cone of Mt. Hood glowing faintly pink on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Portland at dusk, with Mt. Hood on the horizon.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'portland-downtown.jpg'} alt="Close view of the downtown Portland towers lit at dusk, with forested hills in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown, as the lights came on.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    It was not gone from the mountain. Mt. Hood is about fifty miles east of downtown, and from up here it sits
    exactly on the horizon, a white cone over a line of blue hills. At that hour it was the last thing still catching
    the sun, glowing a faint pink while the city below turned blue and the office towers lit up one window at a time.
    That was the second time we had seen the same mountain that day: in the morning from its foot, half buried in
    cloud, and in the evening from fifty miles away, perfectly clear. Then we turned south for the coast.
  </p>

  <h2>Day 5: Down the south coast</h2>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-1201.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-1201.jpg'} alt="Map of the route: Day 5, from North Bend south along the Oregon coast to Face Rock in Bandon, US-101 near Cape Sebastian and the Natural Bridges viewpoint." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 5: the Oregon coast in a morning, before the long drive to Napa.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Morning on Coos Bay</h3>
  <p>
    We woke up in <strong>North Bend</strong>, on Coos Bay, which is the largest deep-water harbor on the coast
    between San Francisco Bay and Puget Sound, and has been shipping Oregon's timber out to the world for a century and
    a half. The town sits on a hill above the bay. At ten to eight the sun was just up behind us, and the view west
    over the rooftops was the whole bay at once: wide tidal water going silver, the low forested ridge of the North
    Spit beyond it, and, past that, the dunes that run north along this coast for forty miles.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'coos-bay-sunrise.jpg'} alt="Early morning over Coos Bay: pale water and tidal flats, a low forested spit, a wide sky with wispy clouds, and a bare tree lit gold by the rising sun." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Coos Bay just after sunrise.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'coos-bay-morning.jpg'} alt="Tall conifers in front of a wide view over Coos Bay and its tidal flats under streaky clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The bay from the hill in North Bend.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The first light did something lovely to a big bare tree in the foreground, turning every branch gold for a few
    minutes, while the clouds above stretched across the whole sky in long feathery streaks. Forty minutes later the
    gold was gone, the bay had turned a cooler blue, and the streaks had become broad brushstrokes. It was a clear
    December morning on a coast better known for fog, and we had a long way to go.
  </p>

  <h3>Face Rock, Bandon</h3>
  <p>
    Half an hour south is <strong>Bandon</strong>, and just outside town, Beach Loop Drive runs along the top of a
    bluff above one of the most crowded stretches of rock on the Oregon coast. We stopped at the overlook at
    <strong>Face Rock State Scenic Viewpoint</strong> a little after ten. The headland is wrapped in gorse, a spiny
    shrub with bright yellow flowers that blooms here even in December, and below it a long beach of dark sand curves
    away with sea stacks scattered in the surf, as if someone had emptied a bag of them along the shore.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bandon-gorse.jpg'} alt="Yellow-flowered gorse on a bluff above a sandy beach, with sea stacks standing in the surf under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Gorse in bloom above the beach at Bandon.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bandon-face-rock.jpg'} alt="A dark headland and a domed sea stack offshore, waves washing onto a sandy beach, dry grass in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The view south from the Face Rock overlook.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The gorse has a history here. Bandon's founder, an Irishman named George Bennett, is said to have brought it over
    from home in the 1870s to remind him of Ireland. It loved the climate and took over the coast. It is also full of
    oil and burns like kindling, and in 1936, when a forest fire reached the gorse around town, almost all of
    Bandon burned down with it. The town was rebuilt; the gorse came back too, and it is still there, blooming cheerfully
    along the top of the bluff.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bandon-overlook.jpg'} alt="The grassy overlook at Bandon with a wooden fence, looking north along the beach to sea stacks silhouetted against a silver sky under heavy cloud." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Looking north from the overlook, into the light.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Turn around and the same coast looks completely different. Looking north, into the light, the clouds were a
    heavy dappled ceiling, the sea was silver, and the rocks along the beach stood up as black silhouettes, one after
    another, toward the town. The overlook itself is a grassy little park with a wooden fence along the
    edge and a paved path to the end of the point, and at that hour on a Monday in December we nearly had it to
    ourselves.
  </p>
  <p>
    Wangcai, as usual, was more interested in the ground than the view. The point has an interpretive sign about the
    rock that gives the place its name, and the photo catches her nose-down behind it, reading it from the
    bottom up. The story on it is a local legend. <strong>Face Rock</strong> is said to be Ewauna, a chief's daughter who
    swam out into the sea against her father's warning, was caught by the evil spirit of the ocean and turned to
    stone. Her face is still out there, turned up to the sky, refusing to look at him, with her cat and kittens
    scattered on the rocks around her.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-bandon.jpg'} alt="A cream-colored dog on a leash sniffing behind an interpretive sign on a grassy overlook, with a dark headland and a sea stack beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai, nose-down behind the sign at Face Rock.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    From the end of the point you look straight down on the rocks. A big dark headland stands just offshore with the
    surf churning through the channel in front of it, and a smaller stack beside it is sharp as a fin. The sea was
    rough that morning, and each wave broke white around the base of the rocks and ran in long curls of foam across
    the channel. Further out, a line of smaller stacks sat on the horizon like a breakwater.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bandon-headland.jpg'} alt="A large dark rock headland in churning surf, with a domed sea stack and a row of smaller rocks on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The headland below the overlook.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bandon-sea-stacks.jpg'} alt="Close view of a dark flat-topped sea stack and pointed rocks in white surf below a cliff edge." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sea stacks in the surf.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Sea stacks are what a cliff leaves behind. The waves find the weak spots in a headland, carve them into caves,
    then arches, and when the arch falls, the hard rock on the seaward side is left standing alone in the water. This
    coast is made of a jumble of hard and soft rock, which is why it erodes so unevenly and why the beaches here are
    so crowded with stacks. Every one of them is a piece of the shoreline as it used to be, and the surf is still
    working on all of them.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bandon-beach.jpg'} alt="A wide empty beach of dark sand curving north below a grassy bluff, with sea stacks in the surf and houses on the bluff top." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The beach north of Face Rock, with nobody on it.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The last photo from the overlook looks north along the beach, empty at ten in the morning on a Monday, with the line of
    stacks marching away up the shore toward town. It is the kind of beach you would happily spend a day walking. The photos from the overlook span about four minutes, and
    the next ones were taken an hour and a half down US-101.
  </p>

  <h3>The Curry County coast</h3>
  <p>
    South of Bandon, US-101 swings inland through farmland and forest, and for a while the ocean disappears. It comes
    back with a vengeance in Curry County, the state's southernmost stretch of coast. Just south of Cape Sebastian,
    at 11:28, the road drops down near sea level, and the windshield filled with a view that looked unreal: the sun
    high over the water ahead, the sea burning silver, and a whole procession of sea stacks lined up in silhouette
    along the shore.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'curry-101.jpg'} alt="US-101 curving along the coast with large sea stacks silhouetted against bright silver water and a dappled sky, a pickup parked at a turnout." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>US-101 south of Cape Sebastian, 11:28 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'curry-sea-stacks.jpg'} alt="Backlit sea stacks standing in shining water beyond a highway guardrail, under a sky of rippled clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The same stacks, a few seconds later.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Both photos were taken through the windshield, a few seconds apart, and they look almost like black-and-white
    pictures: the shapes of the rocks cut out of bright water, the cloud above rippled like sand on a beach. In
    person the effect was the same. With the sun in front of you, you don't see the color of anything, only shapes and
    light, and the coast for a few minutes looked like an old engraving of itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    A few miles further on, the highway enters the <strong>Samuel H. Boardman State Scenic Corridor</strong>, a
    twelve-mile strip of forested cliffs, coves and arches named after the first superintendent of Oregon's state
    parks, who spent decades buying up this coastline so it would stay open to the public. Its most famous stop is
    <strong>Natural Bridges</strong>. A short walk from the parking area leads to a railing on the edge of the forest,
    and below it, down in a cove of dark rock, the ocean pours in and out under a bridge of stone.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'natural-bridges.jpg'} alt="Looking down through tall Sitka spruce to a narrow cove where the ocean surges under a natural stone arch, with the open Pacific beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Natural Bridges, in the Boardman corridor, 11:43 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The bridges are what remains of a sea cave whose roof collapsed. The ocean got in, ate out the soft rock, and
    left the harder roof standing across the gap. From the railing you look down through tall, wind-bent spruce, the
    sun coming in sideways through the branches, to white water surging under the arch and draining back out with
    every wave. Beyond it there is nothing but the Pacific, blue all the way to the horizon. It was the last stop on
    the Oregon coast, and the last photo for almost twelve hours.
  </p>

  <h2>The long drive to Napa</h2>
  <p>
    From Natural Bridges, Napa is still well over three hundred miles away, through the far northwest corner of
    California, and the camera roll says nothing about any of it. The next photo was taken at ten past eleven that
    night, after the day's drive from the Oregon coast. It is a picture of Wangcai, who had
    been in the car for most of the day, curled up into a tight cream-colored ball on the bed with her nose tucked
    into her paw, fast asleep.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-napa.jpg'} alt="A cream-colored dog curled up asleep in a tight ball on a white bedspread, nose tucked into her paw." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai in Napa, 11:10 pm, done for the day.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    That is where the trip came apart. I had a meeting to get back for, so the next day, December 2, I flew home
    early from Oakland. The others
    did the last leg without me: my wife and our friends drove the car, and Wangcai, the rest of the way back to
    Southern California. So in the end the trip was a loop that I never quite closed. I went up the whole length of
    California and Oregon, came back down most of it, and finished in an airplane seat. Wangcai, at least, got to
    see it through to the end.
  </p>

  <h2>A lot of road for five days</h2>
  <p>
    Looking back over the photos, the striking thing is how much of the West fits into one long weekend if you are
    willing to sit in a car for it. There was a desert-country night sky in the Klamath Basin and a volcano's crater
    full of snow-rimmed blue water. Then came a mountain lake in new snow and a city of glass towers
    at dusk with a volcano on its horizon. A bay at sunrise, a beach crowded with sea stacks and a cove where the
    ocean runs under a stone bridge filled the last morning. Between each of them was a long stretch of interstate or
    coast highway, and the photos skip almost all of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is not the way to know any of these places. The camera roll gives Crater Lake two photos and Bandon
    four minutes.
    But each of them is now a particular picture rather than a name: the rim in fresh snow on a bright afternoon, Mt.
    Hood pink over Portland, the silver sea south of Cape Sebastian, a dog sniffing at the foot of a sign about a
    legend. That is enough to want to go back, more slowly, and with fewer miles in a day.
  </p>
  <p class="muted"><small>Maps built from the trip's photo timestamps and locations. Photo locations have been removed
    from the published images. Day maps: map data © OpenStreetMap contributors; each stop is placed where its photo
    was taken, places we stayed are shown only by the name of the nearest town and home is never marked, and the
    dashed lines show the order of stops, not the exact roads.</small></p>
